WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.480
Magnificent, marvelous—legendary.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.520
Undulating as gracefully through the vast&nbsp;
sky above as the watery depths below,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.480
the Chinese dragon is one of the world’s most&nbsp;
globally recognized monsters. Their behavior

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.480
could be described as benevolent—but their&nbsp;
appearance can still inspire fear and awe.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.720
These dragons, or long, as they’re&nbsp;
called in Chinese Mythology,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.520
have been a crucial component of religion,&nbsp;
culture, mythology, and folklore in China for

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.800
thousands of years. The dragon is one of the Four&nbsp;
Benevolent Animals in Chinese mythology and one

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.760
of the twelve members of the Chinese zodiac. And&nbsp;
those are only small parts of their influence.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.440
Dragons played a major role in China’s history.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.040
They were both creators and destroyers,&nbsp;
and controlled the elements.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:48.320
So what was it about the Chinese Dragon that&nbsp;
made it such a central figure in Chinese culture?

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.600
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka, and this is Monstrum

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:59.280
The dragon was born in China a long time ago,&nbsp;
and has appeared throughout ancient and modern

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.160
history. Cave carvings in the Shanxi province&nbsp;
depicting the creature date back to 8,000 BCE.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.760
A Jade Dragon discovered from the Hongshan culture&nbsp;
dates to around 6,000 years ago. And dragon

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.320
heads appear on oracle bones from 1300 BCE. More&nbsp;
recently, Disney’s Shang-Chi gave us a beautiful

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.280
(and mythologically accurate) representation of&nbsp;
the creature. That’s a pretty solid track record.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.720
Wang Fu, a Chinese historian and philosopher&nbsp;
in the Eastern Han Dynasty wrote that the

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.920
dragons’ unique appearance is actually a&nbsp;
composite of many different animal features.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.600
And the list is long! Their heads are the same&nbsp;
shape as a camel, their ears are like a bull,

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.480
and they regard us with eyes similar to a hare&nbsp;
or a murderous demon, depending on the source.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:44.240
Atop their majestic heads grow the antlers of&nbsp;
a stag. They have the writhing body of a snake

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.280
and the well-armored belly of a clam. They have&nbsp;
the feet of a tiger and the enormous talons of

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.040
an eagle. Finally, covering their whole body are&nbsp;
the protective scales of a carp. Some scholars

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.440
believe that these various attributes evolved&nbsp;
out of the influence of the disparate tribes

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.880
that eventually unified into the China we know&nbsp;
today. Others see them as emblematic of desirable

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:10.160
human characteristics or as a representative&nbsp;
amalgamation of ancient spiritual totems.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.200
Some traditions claim that Chinese dragons conceal&nbsp;
a large pearl in their throat or chin. The pearl,

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.520
often depicted in art as a glowing&nbsp;
white disk in a dragon’s claw or mouth,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.320
may represent the sun, the moon, or the&nbsp;
earth. Or maybe it’s just a giant jewel.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.040
Regardless, the dragon’s ability to hold&nbsp;
the precious orb represents immense power.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.480
Even the number of scales on the Chinese&nbsp;
dragon’s body carries important significance.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:42.080
The number is said to be 117 which in traditional&nbsp;
Chinese philosophy and religion represents a

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.000
perfect balance of yin (negative essence) and&nbsp;
yang (positive essence). Because of this natural

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.560
duality, while usually compassionate,&nbsp;
the dragon can be malicious as well.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.480
Or even just really invested in politics. In&nbsp;
the 17th century, scholar Xie Zhaozhe’s text

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.920
The Five Assorted Offerings tells the story of&nbsp;
his own encounter with dragons. According to Xie,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.400
while traveling by sea at 10-years-old with&nbsp;
his uncle on a diplomatic mission, they were

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.320
suddenly plagued by wind, rain, hail, thunder,&nbsp;
and lightning. Three dragons surrounded them.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.480
At first, everyone was terrified&nbsp;
of the extraordinary creatures.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.920
But an elder remarked that they must&nbsp;
be there to hear the Emperor’s message.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.080
They interact with gods and&nbsp;
immortals in the heavens.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.360
Even without wings, they can fly through the&nbsp;
air. They are shapeshifters, capable of taking on

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:37.840
any size or shape—including human form. They can&nbsp;
turn invisible at will, are highly intelligent,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.560
in some stories they can even speak.
The deep meanings attributed to Chinese

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.320
dragons are ancient, so much so that their&nbsp;
exact origins are largely lost to the past.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.360
The evidence that we do have, however,&nbsp;
suggests dragons were a universal facet in

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.560
all early cultures in China even though&nbsp;
traditions and spiritual significance differed.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.440
In The Guideways through Mountains&nbsp;
and Seas, an encyclopedic

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.760
tome and Chinese bestiary penned&nbsp;
between the 4th and 1st century BCE,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.760
the dragon appeared as a nature&nbsp;
deity known as the God of Thunder,

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.520
who uses his own belly to drum up echoing booms.
There were even so-called “Dragon cults,”

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.200
religious groups that worshipped dragons&nbsp;
and gave offerings to appease them.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:25.680
As aquatic, semi-divine beings dragons control&nbsp;
rainstorms, hail, wind, tornados, floods, and all

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.960
bodies of water. Dragons helped explain the&nbsp;
rain cycle during the Song dynasties and

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.280
were thus also said to bring thunder and&nbsp;
lightning. This was an important development

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:40.880
because if humans could summon dragons, then they&nbsp;
had a semblance of control over rain as well.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.280
During this same period, paintings&nbsp;
of dragons dramatically increased.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.200
In Ancient Chinese cosmology there are four kinds&nbsp;
of dragons: the Celestial Dragon, Dragon of Hidden

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.120
Treasure, the underground or underworld dragon&nbsp;
and spiritual Dragon who control rain and winds.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.680
But in other traditions, the taxonomy expands&nbsp;
to nine, an auspicious number in Chinese

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:07.600
numerology—and, interestingly, the same number&nbsp;
of animals the dragon borrows its features from.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.840
Joining the classification system are&nbsp;
the Winged Dragon , the Horned Dragon,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:15.520
the Coiling Dragon, the Yellow&nbsp;
Dragon, and the Dragon King.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:21.120
The Dragon King concept was only introduced after&nbsp;
the&nbsp;arrival of Buddhism in China. When Buddhism

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.800
came to the area, dragons were adopted as a symbol&nbsp;
of&nbsp;enlightenment as well as royalty. Buddhism was

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:32.160
then imported from China to Japan around the&nbsp;
7th century, and dragons came along with it.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.360
Dragons were later incorporated into Shintoism as&nbsp;
well. The Shinto god Susanoo was believed to have

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:41.680
killed an eight-headed dragon—which certainly&nbsp;
seems like a monster-slayer story to me.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:47.360
In Chinese mythology, when the four poles that&nbsp;
held up the sky collapsed one of the disastrous

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.480
consequences was great flooding brought on by&nbsp;
the Black Dragon, a malevolent creature who

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:58.800
could only be stopped by the mother goddess Nüwa.
But other dragons are not so monstrous; after all,

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.320
one of those world-building pillars&nbsp;
was constructed by the Green Dragon,

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.560
one of the deities of creation. In yet&nbsp;
another creation story from the Bai

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.440
peoples, when the world was created, a great&nbsp;
golden dragon lay in slumber under the sea.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.680
When one of two warring suns dropped into&nbsp;
the ocean, the sea began to boil. Awoken and

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:23.840
enraged by this, the golden dragon swallowed the&nbsp;
sun. It began to burn him, eventually bursting

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.360
from his chest and becoming clouds, trees,&nbsp;
flowers, grass, and animals of all kinds.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.680
So to sum it up, not all Chinese dragons are&nbsp;
the same, they serve different purposes. Some

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.960
are celestial, others belong to the river, some&nbsp;
protect treasure, act as deities, or serve as a

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:48.160
connection to the spiritual. Oh, and they can have&nbsp;
human offspring–or more specifically, emperors.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.040
This is one reason why Chinese dragons are&nbsp;
intimately linked to imperial history. During

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.000
the Han dynasty to Qing dynasty period, the&nbsp;
great and powerful dragon became associated

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.360
with emperors as a way to show the rulers had&nbsp;
been divinely chosen. The first Han emperor,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:08.320
known as the Yellow Emperor, was said to be the&nbsp;
offspring of a human mother and a divine Dragon.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.440
This supposed heritage trickled down to all&nbsp;
elements of the emperor’s authority, from their

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.280
robes to their beds. Yellow or golden dragons&nbsp;
were particularly revered by imperial powers.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:25.040
And the five-clawed dragon became an official&nbsp;
imperial symbol in the 14th century. Their claws

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.840
represent the emperor’s power over the five&nbsp;
elements: wood, fire, earth, metal, and water.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.440
Eventually, everyone else was banned from wearing&nbsp;
any clothing that pictured a five-clawed dragon.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:40.880
That’s not to say that all stories of&nbsp;
dragons were based on mythology, animism,

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:47.120
or legend. Ancient animals likely played a role&nbsp;
as well. Yep, we’re talking dragons and fossils.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:54.400
As early as China’s Jin Dynasty, which lasted&nbsp;
from 265 to 317 CE, we find a text mentioning

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.600
the discovery of what were believed to be&nbsp;
dragon bones in what’s now Sichuan Province.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.880
A lot of the sediment in this area dates back&nbsp;
to the middle and late Jurassic period, and the

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:11.520
area is full of fossils. Remains of dinosaurs&nbsp;
like the predatory theropods were found there,

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:17.440
as well as a massive sauropod with an extremely&nbsp;
long neck that could grow up to 25 meters long.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:24.320
Dragons inhabit exotic spaces: mysterious watery&nbsp;
depths, expansive skies, and heavenly realms.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.800
Their powers are frightening but inspirational,

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.760
and they’re beautiful in their&nbsp;
strength and otherworldliness.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.480
Luck, power, wisdom, bravery, strength—Chinese&nbsp;
dragons inspired everything from religious

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:41.920
practices to the national emblem, countless&nbsp;
astounding artworks and architectural marvels

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.960
to the globally famous dragon dance. The&nbsp;
creature is inseparable from the history of

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.920
the Chinese people. To embrace the Chinese&nbsp;
dragon is to celebrate Chinese culture.

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:58.240
Thanks for watching. Let me tell you a little&nbsp;
about Subcultured, a brand new documentary series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:03.280
from PBS that focuses on lesser known communities&nbsp;
and folx that have a major impact on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:08.720
mainstream. What can somebody who is completely&nbsp;
blind teach you about video games? How did anime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:14.880
become a $25 billion dollar industry? Check it out&nbsp;
over on PBS Voices and tell them DR. Z sent you.

